WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:12.236 --> 00:19.036 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference January 31,

00:21.366 --> 01:02.596 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

01:04.416 --> 01:04.926 align:middle
Good afternoon.

01:06.626 --> 01:09.196 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

01:09.196 --> 01:12.106 align:middle
on our dual mandate to
promote maximum employment

01:12.106 --> 01:14.066 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

01:15.336 --> 01:17.456 align:middle
The economy has made
good progress toward our

01:17.456 --> 01:18.726 align:middle
dual-mandate objectives.

01:19.676 --> 01:21.336 align:middle
Inflation has eased
from its highs

01:21.436 --> 01:23.606 align:middle
without a significant
increase in unemployment.

01:24.196 --> 01:25.146 align:middle
That's very good news.

01:26.036 --> 01:29.626 align:middle
But inflation is still too high,
ongoing progress in bringing it

01:29.626 --> 01:33.176 align:middle
down is not assured, and the
path forward is uncertain.

01:34.256 --> 01:36.096 align:middle
I want to assure
the American people

01:36.436 --> 01:38.706 align:middle
that we're fully committed
to returning inflation

01:38.706 --> 01:39.966 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal.

01:41.366 --> 01:43.116 align:middle
Restoring price stability
is essential

01:43.116 --> 01:44.776 align:middle
to achieve a sustained period

01:45.226 --> 01:47.726 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

01:49.906 --> 01:53.276 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy interest rate

01:53.366 --> 01:57.246 align:middle
unchanged and to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

01:58.226 --> 01:59.306 align:middle
Over the past two years,

01:59.306 --> 02:02.316 align:middle
we've significantly tightened
the stance of monetary policy.

02:03.216 --> 02:06.006 align:middle
Our strong actions have
moved our policy rate well

02:06.006 --> 02:09.286 align:middle
into restrictive territory, and
we've been seeing the effects

02:09.336 --> 02:11.816 align:middle
on economic activity
and inflation.

02:13.186 --> 02:15.556 align:middle
As labor market tightness
has eased and progress

02:15.556 --> 02:16.966 align:middle
on inflation has continued,

02:17.156 --> 02:18.976 align:middle
the risks to achieving
our employment

02:18.976 --> 02:21.866 align:middle
and inflation goals are
moving into better balance.

02:22.436 --> 02:24.066 align:middle
I will have more to say

02:24.066 --> 02:26.966 align:middle
about monetary policy-about
monetary policy,

02:26.966 --> 02:29.466 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

02:31.476 --> 02:32.656 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest

02:32.656 --> 02:36.036 align:middle
that economic activity has
been expanding at a solid pace.

02:37.126 --> 02:38.756 align:middle
GDP growth in the fourth quarter

02:38.756 --> 02:41.156 align:middle
of last year came
in at 3.3 percent.

02:42.086 --> 02:46.826 align:middle
For 2023 as a whole, GDP
expanded at 3.1 percent,

02:47.486 --> 02:50.036 align:middle
bolstered by strong
consumer demand as well

02:50.036 --> 02:51.716 align:middle
as improving supply conditions.

02:53.056 --> 02:56.136 align:middle
Activity in the housing sector
was subdued over the past year,

02:56.766 --> 02:59.196 align:middle
largely reflecting
high mortgage rates.

03:00.336 --> 03:02.626 align:middle
High interest rates also
appear to have been weighing

03:02.736 --> 03:04.146 align:middle
on business fixed investment.

03:06.276 --> 03:08.336 align:middle
The labor market
remains tight, but supply

03:08.336 --> 03:11.246 align:middle
and demand conditions continue
to come into better balance.

03:11.876 --> 03:13.716 align:middle
Over the past three months,

03:14.276 --> 03:17.926 align:middle
payroll job gains averaged
165,000 jobs per month,

03:18.566 --> 03:20.086 align:middle
a pace that is well below

03:20.086 --> 03:22.166 align:middle
that seen a year ago
but still strong.

03:23.316 --> 03:26.146 align:middle
The unemployment rate
remains low at 3.7 percent.

03:27.066 --> 03:29.906 align:middle
Strong job creation has been
accompanied by an increase

03:29.906 --> 03:31.066 align:middle
in the supply of workers.

03:31.966 --> 03:34.766 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate has moved up, on balance,

03:34.766 --> 03:39.106 align:middle
over the past year, particularly
for individuals aged 25

03:39.456 --> 03:44.196 align:middle
to 54 years, and immigration has
returned to pre-pandemic levels.

03:45.586 --> 03:47.696 align:middle
Nominal wage growth
has been easing,

03:47.736 --> 03:49.476 align:middle
and job vacancies have declined.

03:50.736 --> 03:52.916 align:middle
Although the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed,

03:53.416 --> 03:56.866 align:middle
labor demand still exceeds the
supply of available workers.

03:58.926 --> 04:02.326 align:middle
Inflation has eased notably
over the past year but remains

04:02.416 --> 04:04.816 align:middle
above our longer-run
goal of 2 percent.

04:06.416 --> 04:08.896 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.6 percent

04:08.896 --> 04:10.716 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in December;

04:11.606 --> 04:13.966 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

04:14.106 --> 04:16.856 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.9 percent.

04:17.636 --> 04:21.166 align:middle
The lower inflation
readings over the second half

04:21.166 --> 04:22.196 align:middle
of last year are welcome.

04:22.196 --> 04:24.966 align:middle
But we will need to
see continuing evidence

04:25.126 --> 04:27.636 align:middle
to build confidence
that inflation is moving

04:27.936 --> 04:29.706 align:middle
down sustainably
toward our goal.

04:31.116 --> 04:33.276 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

04:33.276 --> 04:36.086 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

04:36.086 --> 04:38.546 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

04:39.116 --> 04:41.256 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:43.046 --> 04:45.496 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

04:45.496 --> 04:48.256 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:48.336 --> 04:50.196 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

04:51.236 --> 04:52.966 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

04:52.966 --> 04:55.556 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

04:55.926 --> 04:59.606 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

04:59.606 --> 05:02.236 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

05:02.466 --> 05:03.856 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

05:05.026 --> 05:07.556 align:middle
We're highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

05:07.556 --> 05:10.446 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we're strongly committed

05:10.446 --> 05:12.746 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

05:14.936 --> 05:17.996 align:middle
Over the past two years, we
have raised our policy rate

05:18.046 --> 05:19.696 align:middle
by 5¼ percentage points,

05:20.036 --> 05:21.856 align:middle
and we've decreased
our securities holdings

05:21.926 --> 05:24.276 align:middle
by more than $1.3 trillion.

05:25.246 --> 05:26.166 align:middle
Our restrictive stance

05:26.166 --> 05:28.516 align:middle
of monetary policy is
putting downward pressure

05:28.516 --> 05:30.286 align:middle
on economic activity
and inflation.

05:31.466 --> 05:33.506 align:middle
The Committee decided
at today's meeting

05:34.066 --> 05:37.376 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 5¼

05:37.376 --> 05:41.116 align:middle
to 5½ percent and to
continue the process

05:41.116 --> 05:43.546 align:middle
of significantly reducing
our securities holdings.

05:45.246 --> 05:47.816 align:middle
We believe that our policy
rate is likely at its peak

05:47.816 --> 05:49.616 align:middle
for this tightening
cycle and that,

05:50.146 --> 05:52.446 align:middle
if the economy evolves
broadly as expected,

05:52.976 --> 05:54.366 align:middle
it will likely be appropriate

05:54.366 --> 05:56.556 align:middle
to begin dialing
back policy restraint

05:56.806 --> 05:58.046 align:middle
at some point this year.

05:59.406 --> 06:02.456 align:middle
But the economy has surprised
forecasters in many ways

06:02.526 --> 06:06.086 align:middle
since the pandemic, and ongoing
progress toward our 2 percent

06:06.086 --> 06:08.176 align:middle
inflation objective
is not assured.

06:09.216 --> 06:10.746 align:middle
The economic outlook
is uncertain,

06:11.276 --> 06:13.736 align:middle
and we remain highly
attentive to inflation risks.

06:14.636 --> 06:16.816 align:middle
We're prepared to maintain
the current target range

06:16.816 --> 06:19.266 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
for longer if appropriate.

06:21.186 --> 06:23.406 align:middle
As labor market tightness
has eased and progress

06:23.406 --> 06:24.636 align:middle
on inflation has continued,

06:24.856 --> 06:26.586 align:middle
the risks to achieving
our employment

06:26.586 --> 06:29.406 align:middle
and inflation goals are
moving into better balance.

06:30.486 --> 06:33.096 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too soon

06:33.516 --> 06:36.096 align:middle
or too much could
result in a reversal

06:36.096 --> 06:37.866 align:middle
of the progress we've
seen on inflation

06:38.196 --> 06:40.696 align:middle
and ultimately require
even tighter policy

06:41.086 --> 06:42.866 align:middle
to get inflation
back to 2 percent.

06:44.096 --> 06:47.216 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too late

06:47.216 --> 06:50.716 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

06:50.836 --> 06:51.416 align:middle
and employment.

06:52.936 --> 06:55.226 align:middle
In considering any
adjustments to the target range

06:55.226 --> 06:56.286 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

06:56.776 --> 06:59.106 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess the incoming data,

06:59.596 --> 07:01.716 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

07:02.756 --> 07:05.146 align:middle
The Committee does not expect
that it will be appropriate

07:05.146 --> 07:08.736 align:middle
to reduce the target range until
it has gained greater confidence

07:08.766 --> 07:11.926 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

07:13.326 --> 07:16.406 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting.

07:17.786 --> 07:19.906 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

07:19.906 --> 07:21.176 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

07:21.636 --> 07:24.206 align:middle
and to keeping
longer-run-longer-term inflation

07:24.206 --> 07:25.716 align:middle
expectations well anchored.

07:26.676 --> 07:29.236 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

07:29.576 --> 07:31.386 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

07:31.386 --> 07:33.926 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

07:35.196 --> 07:38.166 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:38.166 --> 07:39.946 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:40.726 --> 07:43.316 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:44.316 --> 07:45.946 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:46.106 --> 07:47.726 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:47.726 --> 07:49.266 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:49.896 --> 07:50.456 align:middle
Thank you.

07:50.626 --> 07:51.836 align:middle
I look forward to our questions.

07:52.266 --> 07:52.966 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:53.876 --> 07:56.286 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

07:56.596 --> 07:58.916 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

07:59.056 --> 08:00.496 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

08:01.346 --> 08:04.856 align:middle
Obviously, in the statement
and just in your remarks there,

08:04.856 --> 08:07.176 align:middle
you note that you don't
want to cut interest rates

08:07.176 --> 08:08.636 align:middle
without greater confidence

08:08.726 --> 08:10.876 align:middle
that inflation is
coming-coming down fully.

08:11.276 --> 08:13.566 align:middle
I wonder-what do you
need to see at this point

08:13.566 --> 08:14.966 align:middle
to gain that confidence?

08:14.966 --> 08:16.546 align:middle
And as you make those decisions,

08:16.546 --> 08:18.646 align:middle
how are you weighing
recent strong growth

08:18.646 --> 08:20.756 align:middle
in consumer spending
data against the sort

08:21.276 --> 08:23.366 align:middle
of solid inflation
progress you've been seeing?

08:23.366 --> 08:23.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:23.616 --> 08:25.016 align:middle
Sorry-say that last part again.

08:25.016 --> 08:25.566 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

08:25.566 --> 08:26.716 align:middle
How are-how are you
weighing the growth data

08:26.716 --> 08:28.676 align:middle
and consumption data, which
have been surprisingly strong,

08:28.676 --> 08:29.696 align:middle
against inflation data?

08:29.696 --> 08:30.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:30.716 --> 08:33.716 align:middle
Okay. So, what are we looking
for to get greater confidence?

08:34.336 --> 08:36.966 align:middle
Let me say that we
have confidence.

08:36.966 --> 08:39.086 align:middle
We're-we're looking
for greater confidence

08:39.086 --> 08:42.176 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably down to 2 percent.

08:42.826 --> 08:44.356 align:middle
Implicitly, we do
have confidence,

08:44.356 --> 08:45.356 align:middle
and it has been increasing,

08:45.356 --> 08:46.946 align:middle
but we want to get
greater confidence.

08:47.246 --> 08:48.076 align:middle
What do we want to see?

08:48.076 --> 08:50.056 align:middle
We want to see more good data.

08:50.056 --> 08:52.576 align:middle
It's not that we're
looking for better data.

08:52.576 --> 08:55.236 align:middle
It's-we're looking at
continuation of the good data

08:55.236 --> 08:57.296 align:middle
that we've been seeing, and
a good example is inflation.

08:57.686 --> 08:59.636 align:middle
So we have six months
of good inflation data.

09:00.026 --> 09:02.216 align:middle
The question really
is, that six months

09:02.216 --> 09:04.756 align:middle
of good inflation data-is
it sending us a true signal

09:05.246 --> 09:09.476 align:middle
that we are, in fact, on
a path-a sustainable path

09:09.476 --> 09:10.666 align:middle
down to 2 percent inflation?

09:10.666 --> 09:11.356 align:middle
That's the question.

09:11.666 --> 09:12.726 align:middle
And the answer will come

09:12.816 --> 09:15.376 align:middle
from some more data
that's also good data.

09:15.876 --> 09:18.446 align:middle
It doesn't-it's not that the
six-month data isn't-isn't

09:18.446 --> 09:18.936 align:middle
low enough.

09:18.936 --> 09:22.236 align:middle
It is. It's just a question of,
can we take that with confidence

09:22.236 --> 09:24.046 align:middle
that we're moving
sustainably down to 2 percent?

09:24.556 --> 09:25.676 align:middle
That's really what
we're thinking about.

09:26.356 --> 09:31.506 align:middle
In terms of, of growth,
we've had strong growth.

09:31.506 --> 09:32.976 align:middle
I mean, if you take a step back,

09:32.976 --> 09:35.516 align:middle
we've had strong growth-very
strong growth [in real GDP] last

09:35.516 --> 09:38.506 align:middle
year-going right into
the fourth quarter.

09:38.766 --> 09:41.136 align:middle
And yet we've had a very
strong labor market,

09:41.306 --> 09:42.906 align:middle
and we've had inflation
coming down.

09:43.656 --> 09:46.446 align:middle
So I think-whereas a year
ago, we, we were thinking

09:46.446 --> 09:47.886 align:middle
that we needed to
see some softening

09:47.886 --> 09:49.996 align:middle
in economic activity-that
hasn't been the case.

09:50.466 --> 09:54.076 align:middle
So I think we, we look at-we
look at stronger growth.

09:54.796 --> 09:55.926 align:middle
We don't look at
it as a problem.

09:55.926 --> 09:57.716 align:middle
I think, at this point, we
want to see strong growth.

09:57.716 --> 09:59.036 align:middle
We want to see a
strong labor market.

09:59.066 --> 10:00.646 align:middle
We're not looking for
a weaker labor market.

10:00.946 --> 10:04.156 align:middle
We're looking for inflation
to continue to come down,

10:04.526 --> 10:06.686 align:middle
as it has been coming down
for the last six months.

10:07.306 --> 10:08.366 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

10:08.366 --> 10:08.696 align:middle
And I'm sorry.

10:08.696 --> 10:11.566 align:middle
If I could just follow up very
quickly, the-when, when you say

10:12.096 --> 10:14.006 align:middle
that you want to make sure
that it's a true signal,

10:14.006 --> 10:15.466 align:middle
is there anything that
you're seeing in the data

10:15.466 --> 10:17.906 align:middle
that makes you doubt that it's
a true signal at this stage?

10:18.076 --> 10:18.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:18.476 --> 10:21.856 align:middle
No, I think it's-I, I would
say it, it seems-it seems

10:21.856 --> 10:25.076 align:middle
to be the likely case that, that
we will achieve that confidence,

10:25.076 --> 10:26.866 align:middle
but we have to achieve
it, and we haven't yet.

10:27.256 --> 10:29.816 align:middle
And so-I mean, it's
a good story.

10:29.816 --> 10:31.196 align:middle
We have six months of
good inflation [readings].

10:31.656 --> 10:34.966 align:middle
But you can-and you know
this-you can look behind those

10:34.966 --> 10:37.116 align:middle
numbers, and you can see that
a lot of it's been coming

10:37.116 --> 10:38.796 align:middle
from goods inflation,
for example,

10:39.286 --> 10:41.476 align:middle
and goods inflation running
significantly negative.

10:41.686 --> 10:43.796 align:middle
It's a reasonable
assumption that, over time,

10:44.086 --> 10:46.886 align:middle
goods inflation will flatten
out-probably approximate zero.

10:47.226 --> 10:48.926 align:middle
That would mean the
services sectors would have

10:48.926 --> 10:49.756 align:middle
to contribute more.

10:50.216 --> 10:52.766 align:middle
So, in other words, what we care

10:52.766 --> 10:54.016 align:middle
about is the aggregate
number-not

10:54.016 --> 10:55.136 align:middle
so much the composition.

10:55.586 --> 10:57.576 align:middle
But we, we just need
to see more.

10:57.576 --> 10:59.486 align:middle
That's where we are,
as a Committee.

10:59.776 --> 11:01.456 align:middle
We need to see more
evidence that sort

11:01.456 --> 11:04.726 align:middle
of confirms what we think
we're seeing and that tells us

11:04.726 --> 11:07.146 align:middle
that we are on-gives us
confidence that we're on,

11:07.686 --> 11:10.946 align:middle
on a path to-a sustainable path
down to 2 percent inflation.

11:12.576 --> 11:13.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:14.926 --> 11:16.826 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

11:16.826 --> 11:18.096 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

11:18.776 --> 11:20.996 align:middle
Chair Powell, it seems to
me you raised rates rapidly

11:20.996 --> 11:22.706 align:middle
over the last two
years for two reasons.

11:22.706 --> 11:25.186 align:middle
One was the risk of
a wage-price spiral.

11:25.216 --> 11:26.586 align:middle
Two, there were risks

11:26.586 --> 11:29.326 align:middle
of inflation expectations
becoming unanchored.

11:29.876 --> 11:31.256 align:middle
This morning's ECI report

11:31.256 --> 11:33.806 align:middle
for the fourth quarter shows
private-sector payroll growth

11:33.806 --> 11:35.796 align:middle
running at a sub-4 percent pace.

11:36.256 --> 11:38.336 align:middle
Inflation expectations
are very close

11:38.336 --> 11:40.646 align:middle
to where they were before
the inflation emergency

11:40.646 --> 11:42.056 align:middle
of the last three years.

11:42.056 --> 11:43.046 align:middle
And, given that you appear

11:43.046 --> 11:46.206 align:middle
to have substantially cut
off these two tail risks

11:46.586 --> 11:49.546 align:middle
and that you've judged here
today current policy as well

11:49.546 --> 11:52.276 align:middle
into restrictive territory,
what good reason is there

11:52.276 --> 11:54.306 align:middle
to keep policy rates
above 5 percent?

11:54.306 --> 11:56.976 align:middle
Are you really going to
learn more waiting six weeks

11:56.976 --> 11:58.256 align:middle
versus three months from now

11:58.596 --> 12:00.946 align:middle
that you have avoided
those two risks?

12:01.706 --> 12:02.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:02.506 --> 12:11.066 align:middle
So, as you know,
almost every participant

12:11.066 --> 12:13.586 align:middle
on the Committee does believe
that it will be appropriate

12:13.586 --> 12:16.066 align:middle
to reduce rates and for,

12:16.346 --> 12:18.326 align:middle
for-partly for the
reasons that you say.

12:18.396 --> 12:21.646 align:middle
You know, we, we feel like
inflation is coming down.

12:22.076 --> 12:23.216 align:middle
Growth has been strong.

12:23.216 --> 12:25.316 align:middle
The labor market is strong.

12:26.156 --> 12:29.426 align:middle
We're-what we're trying
to do is identify a place

12:29.426 --> 12:32.296 align:middle
where we're really confident
about inflation getting back

12:32.296 --> 12:36.066 align:middle
down to 2 percent so that we
can then begin the process

12:36.066 --> 12:38.776 align:middle
of dialing back the
restrictive level.

12:38.776 --> 12:41.996 align:middle
So, overall, I think-I
think people do believe-and,

12:41.996 --> 12:43.536 align:middle
as you know, the
median participant wrote

12:43.536 --> 12:45.606 align:middle
down three rate cuts this year.

12:46.346 --> 12:48.686 align:middle
But I think to get to that place

12:48.686 --> 12:50.406 align:middle
where we feel comfortable
starting the process,

12:50.406 --> 12:52.796 align:middle
we need some confirmation
that inflation is, in fact,

12:52.796 --> 12:55.666 align:middle
coming down sustainably
to 2 percent.

12:55.776 --> 12:56.306 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:56.306 --> 12:59.266 align:middle
If I could ask differently:
If, if you hold rates high

12:59.266 --> 13:01.626 align:middle
as inflation moderates,
as it-as it has been,

13:02.046 --> 13:04.016 align:middle
target rates will
exceed the prescriptions

13:04.016 --> 13:05.986 align:middle
of the Taylor rule
or its variants.

13:06.046 --> 13:08.456 align:middle
What would be the reasoning
for holding rates higher

13:09.016 --> 13:11.366 align:middle
than the levels recommended
by those rules

13:11.726 --> 13:13.076 align:middle
in the current instance?

13:13.076 --> 13:13.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:13.286 --> 13:15.986 align:middle
Well, I-look, I think, as you
know, we consult the range

13:15.986 --> 13:18.526 align:middle
of Taylor rules and, and
non-Taylor kind of rules.

13:19.066 --> 13:20.196 align:middle
We consult them regularly.

13:20.196 --> 13:21.756 align:middle
They're in our, our
Tealbook, and,

13:21.756 --> 13:24.396 align:middle
and they're in all the
materials that we look at.

13:24.426 --> 13:27.546 align:middle
But, you know, I don't think
we've ever been at a-at a place

13:27.546 --> 13:30.666 align:middle
where we were-where we were
setting policy by them.

13:30.666 --> 13:32.606 align:middle
And there-depending on the rule,

13:32.876 --> 13:34.206 align:middle
it will tell you
different things.

13:34.206 --> 13:35.496 align:middle
There are many different
formulations.

13:36.166 --> 13:40.416 align:middle
Another way to think about
it is, implicitly, is-so,

13:40.856 --> 13:43.716 align:middle
in theory, of course,
real rates go

13:43.716 --> 13:46.556 align:middle
up if-holding all else
equal-as inflation comes down.

13:47.116 --> 13:50.766 align:middle
But that doesn't mean we can
mechanically adjust policy

13:51.236 --> 13:54.046 align:middle
as real rates-sorry-as
inflation comes down.

13:54.046 --> 13:56.856 align:middle
It doesn't mean that at all,
because, for one thing, we,

13:56.996 --> 13:58.816 align:middle
we don't know-we,
we look at more

13:58.816 --> 13:59.886 align:middle
than just the fed funds rate.

13:59.886 --> 14:01.976 align:middle
We look at-broadly-financial
conditions.

14:02.336 --> 14:04.526 align:middle
But, in addition, we don't
know with great confidence

14:04.526 --> 14:06.576 align:middle
where the neutral rate of
interest is at any given time.

14:07.116 --> 14:11.436 align:middle
But that also doesn't mean
that we wait around for-to see,

14:11.656 --> 14:14.146 align:middle
you know, the economy turn down,
because that would be too late.

14:14.226 --> 14:16.156 align:middle
So we're really in a
risk-management mode:

14:16.546 --> 14:18.246 align:middle
of managing the risk-as
I mentioned

14:18.246 --> 14:20.076 align:middle
in my opening remarks-managing
the risk

14:20.076 --> 14:22.276 align:middle
that we move too soon
and move too late.

14:22.746 --> 14:24.766 align:middle
And I think to move,
which is-which is

14:24.766 --> 14:28.806 align:middle
where almost everyone on the
Committee is-is in favor of,

14:29.036 --> 14:32.116 align:middle
of moving rates down
this year-but the timing

14:32.116 --> 14:35.526 align:middle
of that is going to be linked
to our gaining confidence

14:35.526 --> 14:36.636 align:middle
that inflation is

14:36.906 --> 14:38.496 align:middle
on a sustainable path
down to 2 percent.

14:38.496 --> 14:40.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:40.266 --> 14:43.046 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

14:43.046 --> 14:43.876 align:middle
Hi. Thanks, Chair Powell.

14:43.876 --> 14:46.716 align:middle
I'd like you to, to key in
on the use of the word in,

14:46.716 --> 14:50.366 align:middle
in the statement that inflation
still "remains elevated."

14:51.266 --> 14:53.886 align:middle
You've pledged to cut rates
before inflation reached

14:53.886 --> 14:54.536 align:middle
2 percent.

14:55.056 --> 14:56.846 align:middle
So that implies that
there's some sort

14:56.846 --> 14:59.766 align:middle
of intermediate step here
on, on inflation and that a,

14:59.766 --> 15:03.296 align:middle
a cut would be consequent with a
change in the statement language

15:03.296 --> 15:05.456 align:middle
that inflation "remains
elevated."

15:05.826 --> 15:07.186 align:middle
What's the step-down from there?

15:07.316 --> 15:08.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:08.026 --> 15:10.366 align:middle
Yeah, I, I don't know
that we've worked

15:10.366 --> 15:12.836 align:middle
out the particulars-statement
language and that kind of thing.

15:12.836 --> 15:14.746 align:middle
I would just say, if you look
at-you know, look at where,

15:14.836 --> 15:18.336 align:middle
where 12-month inflation
is, and it's, you know,

15:18.336 --> 15:21.006 align:middle
it's still well above-core
is 2.9 percent,

15:21.006 --> 15:23.546 align:middle
for example-12-month-which is
way down from where it was.

15:23.546 --> 15:26.126 align:middle
Very, very positive
development-very fast

15:26.126 --> 15:29.396 align:middle
decline-and, and, you know,
the, the case is likely

15:29.396 --> 15:30.526 align:middle
that it will continue
to come down.

15:30.936 --> 15:32.876 align:middle
So, so that's where-that's
where it is.

15:33.316 --> 15:34.736 align:middle
But we're, you know,
we're wanting

15:34.736 --> 15:36.806 align:middle
to see, you know, more data.

15:36.806 --> 15:38.216 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:38.436 --> 15:41.336 align:middle
So, if I-if I could follow up
on that, the statement allows

15:41.336 --> 15:42.766 align:middle
that you want greater confidence

15:42.836 --> 15:45.276 align:middle
on inflation falling
before you cut,

15:45.276 --> 15:47.266 align:middle
but it doesn't mention
the other side

15:47.266 --> 15:49.316 align:middle
of the mandate-a
slide in employment.

15:49.316 --> 15:52.446 align:middle
Would a slide in
employment also bring you

15:52.446 --> 15:53.896 align:middle
to the point of,
of cutting rates?

15:53.896 --> 15:54.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:54.676 --> 15:57.796 align:middle
Yes. So let me say that
we're not looking for that.

15:57.796 --> 15:59.096 align:middle
That's not something
we're looking for.

15:59.096 --> 16:02.376 align:middle
But, yes, if you think about,
you know-in, in the base case,

16:02.616 --> 16:06.146 align:middle
the economy is performing
well-the labor market

16:06.146 --> 16:06.756 align:middle
remains strong.

16:07.576 --> 16:11.676 align:middle
If we saw an unexpected
weakening in, in-certainly

16:11.676 --> 16:13.536 align:middle
in the labor market, that
would certainly weigh

16:13.536 --> 16:14.536 align:middle
on cutting sooner.

16:15.406 --> 16:16.006 align:middle
Absolutely.

16:16.006 --> 16:19.756 align:middle
And if we saw inflation
being stickier or higher

16:19.756 --> 16:24.416 align:middle
or those sorts of things-would
argue for moving later.

16:24.416 --> 16:27.866 align:middle
In the base case, though, where,
where the economy is healthy

16:28.256 --> 16:32.316 align:middle
and we have, you know, we have
ongoing growth-solid growth-we

16:32.316 --> 16:34.976 align:middle
have a strong labor market-we
have inflation coming

16:34.976 --> 16:37.186 align:middle
down-that's what people
are writing their SEP

16:37.186 --> 16:37.706 align:middle
[submissions] around.

16:38.306 --> 16:41.336 align:middle
And in that case, what we're
saying is, based on that,

16:41.336 --> 16:45.026 align:middle
we think we can and should
take advantage of that and,

16:45.026 --> 16:47.436 align:middle
and be careful as we approach
that question of when to begin

16:47.436 --> 16:48.396 align:middle
to dial back restriction.

16:50.066 --> 16:50.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:52.406 --> 16:53.406 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

16:53.496 --> 16:55.086 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

16:55.086 --> 16:59.656 align:middle
Just to circle back to the
"greater confidence" aspect

16:59.656 --> 17:01.816 align:middle
of the statement,
there's been a lot

17:01.816 --> 17:04.556 align:middle
of unanimity in recent meetings.

17:04.966 --> 17:07.256 align:middle
I'm just wondering, going
forward, when it comes

17:07.356 --> 17:09.776 align:middle
to all needing greater
confidence,

17:10.816 --> 17:14.826 align:middle
is there unanimity or, at least,
consensus among FOMC members

17:15.246 --> 17:19.076 align:middle
about what the threshold for
that greater confidence is?

17:19.716 --> 17:22.906 align:middle
And if not, could you
maybe tell us a little bit

17:22.906 --> 17:27.366 align:middle
about the discussion today on,
you know, what the variations

17:27.366 --> 17:32.196 align:middle
between FOMC members was on what
constitutes enough confidence

17:32.616 --> 17:37.216 align:middle
to cut rates and also if there
was any variation on how quickly

17:37.766 --> 17:41.316 align:middle
that "greater confidence"
threshold could be reached?

17:41.586 --> 17:41.906 align:middle
Thank you.

17:41.906 --> 17:42.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:42.116 --> 17:43.976 align:middle
So we're not-we're not
really at that stage.

17:44.166 --> 17:47.746 align:middle
You know, we're-we're-there
was no proposal to cut rates.

17:48.256 --> 17:49.976 align:middle
Some people did, you know,

17:49.976 --> 17:52.016 align:middle
talk about their view
of the rate path.

17:52.196 --> 17:55.056 align:middle
I would point you to the
[December 2023] SEP as, as,

17:55.156 --> 17:57.566 align:middle
you know, as good evidence
of where people are,

17:57.566 --> 17:59.396 align:middle
although it is-it is [now] one
[FOMC meeting] cycle later.

17:59.936 --> 18:04.046 align:middle
So, you know, we're
not-we're not at a place of,

18:04.046 --> 18:05.916 align:middle
of really working out
those kinds of details,

18:05.916 --> 18:08.726 align:middle
because we weren't actively
considering, you know, a-moving,

18:09.106 --> 18:11.426 align:middle
moving the federal
funds rate down.

18:11.796 --> 18:14.796 align:middle
I will say, there is a-there
is a wide disparity-a healthy

18:14.796 --> 18:18.366 align:middle
disparity-of views, and
you see that in public,

18:18.456 --> 18:21.886 align:middle
public statements, in the
minutes, and the transcripts

18:21.886 --> 18:23.226 align:middle
when they're released
every five years.

18:23.226 --> 18:26.126 align:middle
So we do have a healthy
set of differences,

18:26.126 --> 18:27.716 align:middle
and I think that's
actually essential

18:27.716 --> 18:29.306 align:middle
for making good policy.

18:29.606 --> 18:32.386 align:middle
We're also able to reach
agreement, generally,

18:32.386 --> 18:35.096 align:middle
because we listen to each
other-we, we compromise.

18:35.096 --> 18:37.866 align:middle
And even though not
everybody loves what we do,

18:37.866 --> 18:39.936 align:middle
they're able to-for the
most part-able to join in.

18:39.936 --> 18:42.356 align:middle
To me, that's a well-functioning
public institution.

18:43.066 --> 18:43.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:43.756 --> 18:46.216 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

18:47.866 --> 18:48.596 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

18:48.596 --> 18:50.066 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

18:50.216 --> 18:51.386 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

18:51.976 --> 18:53.396 align:middle
So, over the past few years,

18:53.426 --> 18:55.646 align:middle
there have been all these
real-time indicators

18:55.646 --> 18:57.466 align:middle
that helped us gain a
sharper understanding

18:57.466 --> 18:58.826 align:middle
of where the economy was,

18:58.946 --> 19:01.276 align:middle
like OpenTable data
or office attendance.

19:01.416 --> 19:03.236 align:middle
You've talked about
vacancies in the past.

19:03.646 --> 19:06.416 align:middle
And I'm wondering, at the start
of this year, what might be

19:06.416 --> 19:08.916 align:middle
on that dashboard for you that's
giving you the clearest picture

19:08.916 --> 19:10.306 align:middle
of the economy-including

19:10.306 --> 19:11.426 align:middle
on rents-if you could
touch on that.

19:11.426 --> 19:12.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:12.036 --> 19:12.466 align:middle
Including?

19:12.626 --> 19:13.816 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

19:13.816 --> 19:14.996 align:middle
Rent. Rent costs.

19:15.096 --> 19:15.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:15.746 --> 19:18.756 align:middle
Yeah. Well, so we're not-you
know, it's not the pandemic,

19:18.756 --> 19:21.966 align:middle
so we can actually rely on
more, more traditional forms.

19:21.966 --> 19:22.866 align:middle
People are working.

19:22.866 --> 19:26.596 align:middle
They're getting wages, and, and
the economy has largely reopened

19:26.596 --> 19:28.656 align:middle
and is broadly normalizing,
as you see.

19:29.126 --> 19:30.776 align:middle
So I wouldn't say
we're looking at that,

19:30.806 --> 19:33.526 align:middle
that sort of more
innovative data as much.

19:34.096 --> 19:35.716 align:middle
You know, you point to rents.

19:35.716 --> 19:38.016 align:middle
So, of course, we follow
the, the components

19:38.016 --> 19:39.356 align:middle
of inflation very carefully.

19:39.956 --> 19:42.056 align:middle
Which would be: Goods
inflation-I talked

19:42.056 --> 19:44.446 align:middle
about that a little bit; you
mentioned housing inflation.

19:44.446 --> 19:48.636 align:middle
So the question is, when will
these lower market rents find

19:48.636 --> 19:51.136 align:middle
their way into measured
rents, as measured,

19:51.166 --> 19:52.536 align:middle
measured in PCE inflation?

19:52.536 --> 19:54.896 align:middle
And we think that's coming,
and we know it's coming.

19:54.896 --> 19:57.166 align:middle
It's just a question of when
and, and how big it'll be.

19:57.166 --> 19:59.936 align:middle
So-but that's in, in everyone's
forecast, I would say.

20:00.656 --> 20:01.836 align:middle
So that will-that will help.

20:01.836 --> 20:03.836 align:middle
But at the same time, we
think goods inflation will

20:03.836 --> 20:06.906 align:middle
probably-it's been giving
a lot of disinflation

20:06.906 --> 20:09.336 align:middle
to the effort-and probably
that declines over time,

20:09.336 --> 20:11.666 align:middle
but it may well have some,
some more time to run.

20:12.036 --> 20:14.976 align:middle
You know, these-the supply
chains are not perfectly back

20:14.976 --> 20:15.596 align:middle
to where they were.

20:15.596 --> 20:18.066 align:middle
In addition, it takes
time for the,

20:18.066 --> 20:20.006 align:middle
the healing process
to get into prices.

20:20.006 --> 20:21.746 align:middle
So there may be still
a tailwind.

20:21.746 --> 20:23.136 align:middle
We'll find out with, with that.

20:23.136 --> 20:24.996 align:middle
So we look at the
things that relate

20:24.996 --> 20:27.656 align:middle
to our mandate very carefully,
and-as you would imagine.

20:27.776 --> 20:28.076 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

20:28.496 --> 20:30.156 align:middle
I guess, just as a quick
follow-up-do you feel

20:30.156 --> 20:32.846 align:middle
comfortable at this point saying
the economy has reached a soft

20:32.846 --> 20:35.466 align:middle
landing, or is that part of
looking for more confidence?

20:35.696 --> 20:36.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:36.096 --> 20:37.886 align:middle
No, I wouldn't-I wouldn't
say we've achieved that.

20:37.886 --> 20:40.236 align:middle
And I, I think we
have-we have a ways to go.

20:40.236 --> 20:43.496 align:middle
Inflation is still-you know,
core inflation is still well

20:43.496 --> 20:44.896 align:middle
above target on a
12-month basis.

20:44.896 --> 20:47.476 align:middle
Twelve months is our,
our target, certainly.

20:47.476 --> 20:49.806 align:middle
I'm encouraged, and we're
encouraged by the progress.

20:50.456 --> 20:51.126 align:middle
But, you know, we're,

20:51.176 --> 20:54.606 align:middle
we're not-we're not declaring
victory at all at this point.

20:54.606 --> 20:55.576 align:middle
We think we have a ways to go.

20:56.076 --> 20:56.776 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:56.976 --> 20:59.516 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

20:59.636 --> 21:00.946 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

21:02.036 --> 21:05.206 align:middle
You've said that you would know
the neutral rate by its works.

21:05.266 --> 21:08.756 align:middle
So I'm wondering what you could
tell me-how do you believe the

21:08.756 --> 21:11.696 align:middle
neutral rate is working
or telling you right now

21:11.696 --> 21:12.906 align:middle
that growth is stronger?

21:12.906 --> 21:17.876 align:middle
In other words, how much is the
economy really being restrained

21:17.876 --> 21:19.346 align:middle
right now by the
current funds rate?

21:20.156 --> 21:23.196 align:middle
And how much restraint does
it really need, additionally,

21:23.196 --> 21:24.826 align:middle
if inflation is still
coming down?

21:25.026 --> 21:25.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:25.896 --> 21:29.526 align:middle
So it's-I think you, you do see
in the interest-sensitive parts

21:29.526 --> 21:31.676 align:middle
of the economy-you do
see, for example, housing.

21:31.736 --> 21:32.656 align:middle
You see the effects.

21:32.786 --> 21:35.826 align:middle
You do. Your, your second
question, though, really,

21:35.826 --> 21:37.346 align:middle
I think, is important,
and that is,

21:38.056 --> 21:39.946 align:middle
a lot of this has
come through-a lot

21:40.396 --> 21:44.196 align:middle
of the disinflationary process
has come through the healing

21:44.576 --> 21:46.806 align:middle
of supply chains and
also of the labor market.

21:47.116 --> 21:49.056 align:middle
So you've seen the-you
know, that other set

21:49.056 --> 21:52.356 align:middle
of factors is really
different from other cycles

21:52.706 --> 21:56.376 align:middle
and has brought that working
with tighter, tighter policy,

21:56.376 --> 21:59.166 align:middle
which has enabled the supply
side to recover-I think is that,

21:59.226 --> 22:02.596 align:middle
that mixture has been behind
what has enabled this.

22:03.156 --> 22:05.036 align:middle
So, no-we really do think

22:05.036 --> 22:07.116 align:middle
that we're having an effect
broadly across the economy.

22:07.116 --> 22:09.326 align:middle
I would point to the
interest-sensitive parts

22:09.736 --> 22:12.916 align:middle
of the economy as well
as spending, generally.

22:13.536 --> 22:15.956 align:middle
But it's a-it's a joint story.

22:15.956 --> 22:16.866 align:middle
It's a complicated story.

22:16.936 --> 22:17.426 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

22:17.426 --> 22:19.776 align:middle
But, but how much restraint
are you actually imparting

22:19.776 --> 22:22.126 align:middle
to the economy, would you say,
relative to the neutral rate?

22:22.296 --> 22:22.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:22.946 --> 22:25.226 align:middle
It's-so I think it's,
it's-of course,

22:25.226 --> 22:27.776 align:middle
you know that it's not
something you can identify

22:27.776 --> 22:28.746 align:middle
with any precision.

22:28.806 --> 22:32.316 align:middle
But if you-a standard
approach would be

22:32.316 --> 22:34.806 align:middle
to take the nominal
rate-5.3 percent,

22:34.806 --> 22:37.306 align:middle
let's say-and subtract
sort of a,

22:37.346 --> 22:38.846 align:middle
a forward measure of inflation.

22:38.846 --> 22:40.216 align:middle
If you do that-and
there are many,

22:40.216 --> 22:43.146 align:middle
many ways to calculate the
neutral rate-but that's one I

22:43.146 --> 22:43.696 align:middle
like to do.

22:44.046 --> 22:45.396 align:middle
And, you know, you're
going to get to something

22:45.396 --> 22:48.596 align:middle
that is materially above
mainstream estimates

22:48.596 --> 22:51.336 align:middle
of neutrality-of the
neutral rate-you will.

22:51.336 --> 22:54.206 align:middle
And-but at the same time,
you look at the economy,

22:54.316 --> 22:55.426 align:middle
and you say, "This is an economy

22:55.426 --> 22:57.606 align:middle
that grew 3.1 percent
last year."

22:58.136 --> 23:00.456 align:middle
And, and you say, "What
does that tell you

23:00.456 --> 23:01.226 align:middle
about the neutral rate?"

23:01.486 --> 23:02.476 align:middle
What's happening, though, is,

23:02.476 --> 23:04.266 align:middle
the supply side has
been recovering

23:04.266 --> 23:05.116 align:middle
in the middle of this.

23:05.716 --> 23:07.856 align:middle
So that, that won't
go on forever.

23:07.856 --> 23:11.536 align:middle
So a lot of the growth we're
seeing is not-is-it isn't just a

23:11.536 --> 23:14.706 align:middle
tug of war between, between
interest rates and demand.

23:14.706 --> 23:17.286 align:middle
You're getting, you
know, more activity

23:17.286 --> 23:20.166 align:middle
because of the-of
labor market healing

23:20.216 --> 23:22.106 align:middle
and supply chains healing.

23:22.146 --> 23:26.886 align:middle
So I think the question is, when
that peters out, I think the,

23:26.886 --> 23:28.926 align:middle
the, you know, the, the
restriction will show

23:28.926 --> 23:30.106 align:middle
up probably more, more sharply.

23:31.086 --> 23:31.496 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:31.636 --> 23:32.966 align:middle
Rich. RICH MILLER.

23:33.986 --> 23:34.616 align:middle
Thank you-sorry.

23:34.616 --> 23:37.726 align:middle
Thanks for taking the
question, Mr. Chairman.

23:39.106 --> 23:41.956 align:middle
You mentioned earlier we're not
seeking a weaker labor market,

23:41.956 --> 23:43.376 align:middle
I think you, you said.

23:43.726 --> 23:46.146 align:middle
Can you talk a little
bit more about that?

23:46.146 --> 23:49.326 align:middle
Do you-do you think the labor
market now is back to quote,

23:49.326 --> 23:57.696 align:middle
unquote, "normal" and that
the-we can achieve the inflation

23:57.696 --> 24:00.406 align:middle
target without wage
gains coming back

24:00.446 --> 24:03.096 align:middle
down to what they
were pre-pandemic?

24:03.096 --> 24:05.026 align:middle
Even with today's ECI
levels, they were still

24:05.026 --> 24:07.216 align:middle
above those pre-pandemic levels.

24:07.306 --> 24:07.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:07.536 --> 24:10.376 align:middle
I, I think the labor market
by many measures is at

24:10.376 --> 24:13.796 align:middle
or nearing normal-but not
totally back to normal.

24:13.986 --> 24:16.596 align:middle
And you pointed to
one or more of them.

24:16.596 --> 24:17.266 align:middle
So I think, you know,

24:17.266 --> 24:19.396 align:middle
job openings are not quite
back to where they were.

24:19.896 --> 24:23.366 align:middle
Wages-wage increases,
rather, are not quite back

24:23.366 --> 24:24.906 align:middle
to where they-to
where they would need

24:24.906 --> 24:25.836 align:middle
to be in the longer run.

24:26.136 --> 24:27.496 align:middle
I, I would look at
it this way, though.

24:27.896 --> 24:31.746 align:middle
The, the economy is
broadly normalizing,

24:31.956 --> 24:33.626 align:middle
and so is the labor market.

24:33.946 --> 24:35.946 align:middle
And that process will
probably take some time.

24:35.946 --> 24:39.176 align:middle
So wage setting is
something that happens-it's,

24:39.176 --> 24:41.916 align:middle
it's-you know, probably
will take a couple of years

24:41.916 --> 24:43.226 align:middle
to get all the way back.

24:43.556 --> 24:44.296 align:middle
And that's okay.

24:44.636 --> 24:45.216 align:middle
That's okay.

24:45.526 --> 24:48.446 align:middle
But we do see-you saw
today's ECI reading-you know,

24:48.446 --> 24:51.426 align:middle
the evidence is that, that
wage increases are still

24:51.426 --> 24:54.486 align:middle
at a healthy level-very healthy
level-but they're gradually

24:54.486 --> 24:58.476 align:middle
moving back to levels that
would be more associated-given,

24:58.476 --> 25:00.076 align:middle
given assumptions
about productivity,

25:00.396 --> 25:02.836 align:middle
are more typically associated
with 2 percent inflation.

25:03.686 --> 25:06.976 align:middle
It's, it's an ongoing process-a
healthy one-and, and, you know,

25:06.976 --> 25:08.606 align:middle
I think we're, we're moving
in the right direction.

25:08.606 --> 25:08.826 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

25:08.826 --> 25:11.196 align:middle
So that process can
continue without a weakening

25:11.196 --> 25:12.786 align:middle
of the labor market,
basically, you're saying?

25:12.786 --> 25:13.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:13.136 --> 25:16.226 align:middle
I think the, the labor
market is-it-I don't know

25:16.226 --> 25:17.416 align:middle
if I'd-it's rebalancing.

25:17.576 --> 25:21.976 align:middle
Clearly, that the-there was
a fairly severe imbalance

25:21.976 --> 25:24.276 align:middle
between demand for
workers and supply

25:24.276 --> 25:25.406 align:middle
at the beginning
of the pandemic.

25:25.766 --> 25:28.226 align:middle
So we lost several million
workers at the beginning

25:28.496 --> 25:31.216 align:middle
of the pandemic from people
dropping out of the labor force.

25:31.566 --> 25:35.786 align:middle
And then when the economy
reopened-you remember 2021-you

25:35.786 --> 25:37.126 align:middle
had a severe labor shortage,

25:37.126 --> 25:39.906 align:middle
and it was just-it was
everywhere-panic on the part

25:39.906 --> 25:41.666 align:middle
of businesses-couldn't,
couldn't find people.

25:42.096 --> 25:45.996 align:middle
So, what's happened is-we
expected labor-the labor

25:46.176 --> 25:49.536 align:middle
supply-labor market to come
back quickly, and it didn't.

25:49.536 --> 25:53.476 align:middle
And 2022 was a disappointing
year, and, you know,

25:53.476 --> 25:55.396 align:middle
we were kind of thinking, "Well,
maybe we won't get it back."

25:55.396 --> 25:56.846 align:middle
And then 2023, we did,

25:56.846 --> 26:00.326 align:middle
as you know-so labor force
participation came back strongly

26:00.716 --> 26:02.636 align:middle
in '23, and so did immigration.

26:02.966 --> 26:05.376 align:middle
Immigration came to a
halt during the pandemic.

26:05.376 --> 26:09.486 align:middle
So-and so those two forces
have significantly lowered the

26:09.486 --> 26:11.276 align:middle
temperature in the labor market

26:11.276 --> 26:13.626 align:middle
to what is still a very
strong labor market.

26:13.626 --> 26:17.266 align:middle
It's still a good labor market
for wages and for finding a job,

26:17.796 --> 26:19.666 align:middle
but it's getting
back into balance,

26:20.226 --> 26:21.446 align:middle
and that's what we want to see.

26:21.446 --> 26:23.026 align:middle
And, you know, one
great way to look

26:23.026 --> 26:26.046 align:middle
at that is what's happening
with, with wage increases.

26:26.046 --> 26:28.896 align:middle
And you see it now across
the, the major things

26:28.896 --> 26:30.026 align:middle
that we-that we track.

26:30.386 --> 26:32.426 align:middle
It isn't every quarter,
but, overall,

26:32.426 --> 26:35.566 align:middle
there's a clear trend-still
at high levels,

26:35.566 --> 26:39.056 align:middle
but back down to where-what
would be consistent with,

26:39.056 --> 26:40.586 align:middle
with where we were
before the pandemic

26:40.586 --> 26:41.716 align:middle
and with 2 percent inflation.

26:43.076 --> 26:43.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:44.126 --> 26:46.116 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

26:46.116 --> 26:47.966 align:middle
Hi, Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

26:48.076 --> 26:48.586 align:middle
Thank you.

26:48.686 --> 26:50.576 align:middle
I wanted to follow up
on Rich's question.

26:50.576 --> 26:54.306 align:middle
It sounded like you suggested
that you're not worried

26:54.306 --> 26:57.496 align:middle
about faster growth so
much-so wanted to see

26:57.496 --> 26:59.416 align:middle
if you're seeing
anything that suggests

26:59.416 --> 27:01.646 align:middle
that inflation could
reaccelerate from here.

27:02.316 --> 27:04.246 align:middle
And it sounds like you're
saying you're not worried

27:04.246 --> 27:08.176 align:middle
that solid growth from here on
out poses any risk to inflation.

27:08.526 --> 27:08.926 align:middle
Thank you.

27:08.926 --> 27:09.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:09.336 --> 27:11.426 align:middle
No, I think that that
is a risk-the risk

27:11.426 --> 27:13.796 align:middle
that inflation would,
would reaccelerate.

27:13.796 --> 27:15.236 align:middle
I think the, the greater risk is

27:15.236 --> 27:17.906 align:middle
that it would-that
it would stabilize

27:17.906 --> 27:19.756 align:middle
at a level meaningfully
above 2 percent.

27:20.176 --> 27:22.226 align:middle
That's, that's, to
me, more likely.

27:22.286 --> 27:28.356 align:middle
Of course, if-if inflation were
to surprise by moving back up,

27:28.416 --> 27:30.406 align:middle
that would-we would
have to respond to that,

27:30.406 --> 27:32.176 align:middle
and that would-that would
be a surprise at this point.

27:32.176 --> 27:33.346 align:middle
But I have to tell you,

27:33.346 --> 27:35.136 align:middle
that's why we keep
our options open here

27:35.346 --> 27:36.886 align:middle
and why we're not,
you know, rushing.

27:37.586 --> 27:40.496 align:middle
So I, I think both
of those are risks,

27:40.646 --> 27:44.016 align:middle
but I think the more likely risk
is the one that I mentioned,

27:44.016 --> 27:48.366 align:middle
which is, you've had six good
months-very good months-but

27:48.626 --> 27:50.656 align:middle
what, what's really going
to "shake out" here?

27:50.656 --> 27:53.636 align:middle
You know, where-what will-when
we look back, what will we see?

27:53.636 --> 27:55.886 align:middle
Will, will inflation have
dipped and then come back up?

27:56.336 --> 27:59.736 align:middle
Are the last six months
flattered by factors

27:59.736 --> 28:01.316 align:middle
that are-that are
one-off factors

28:01.316 --> 28:02.226 align:middle
that won't repeat themselves?

28:02.476 --> 28:03.226 align:middle
We don't think so.

28:03.396 --> 28:04.946 align:middle
We don't-you know,
that's not what we think,

28:04.946 --> 28:06.676 align:middle
but that's the question
we are asking.

28:06.676 --> 28:09.166 align:middle
We have to ask [that], and we
want to get comfort on that.

28:09.166 --> 28:09.966 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

28:10.206 --> 28:12.506 align:middle
And just one quick
follow-up-Governor Waller had

28:12.506 --> 28:15.526 align:middle
mentioned the revisions
that are coming

28:15.526 --> 28:17.106 align:middle
on February 9th for
the CPI data.

28:17.106 --> 28:19.786 align:middle
Is that something
you're watching as well?

28:19.786 --> 28:23.206 align:middle
And if, if we see those
revisions fairly minor,

28:23.326 --> 28:25.076 align:middle
is that going to give
you more confidence

28:25.076 --> 28:26.216 align:middle
where things are going?

28:26.216 --> 28:26.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:26.826 --> 28:27.636 align:middle
We'll just have to see.

28:27.936 --> 28:29.156 align:middle
Yeah. We'll-we look at those.

28:29.556 --> 28:33.236 align:middle
Last year was a-was
a, a surprise.

28:34.226 --> 28:34.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:35.706 --> 28:37.746 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:39.436 --> 28:42.176 align:middle
Michael McKee, Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

28:42.516 --> 28:45.446 align:middle
If you don't want to use
the term "soft landing,"

28:45.576 --> 28:49.636 align:middle
would you say, at least, that,
from your point of view now,

28:49.766 --> 28:53.086 align:middle
the other scenario of
a hard landing caused

28:53.086 --> 28:55.666 align:middle
by the Fed is off the table

28:55.666 --> 28:59.986 align:middle
or the risks have
diminished very much?

28:59.986 --> 29:03.906 align:middle
And you mentioned below-2
percent inflation for-on a

29:03.906 --> 29:07.896 align:middle
three-month basis, core PCE has
been running at 1½ percent.

29:08.276 --> 29:09.926 align:middle
And there are those on
Wall Street who think

29:09.926 --> 29:12.676 align:middle
that if you maintain the level

29:12.676 --> 29:14.906 align:middle
of restriction you have
right now, you could end

29:14.906 --> 29:18.066 align:middle
up with inflation
running below your target.

29:18.466 --> 29:20.256 align:middle
How do you see that?

29:20.676 --> 29:22.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:22.516 --> 29:23.916 align:middle
So, how to-your first
question-how

29:23.916 --> 29:25.876 align:middle
to describe where we are?

29:25.876 --> 29:29.616 align:middle
So I guess I would just say
this-executive summary would be

29:29.616 --> 29:34.026 align:middle
that growth is solid to strong
over the course of last year.

29:34.346 --> 29:40.176 align:middle
The labor market-3.7 percent
unemployment indicates

29:40.176 --> 29:41.706 align:middle
that the labor market is strong.

29:41.706 --> 29:44.476 align:middle
We've had just about
two years now of,

29:44.746 --> 29:46.526 align:middle
of unemployment under 4 percent.

29:46.526 --> 29:48.736 align:middle
That hasn't happened
in 50 years.

29:48.736 --> 29:50.006 align:middle
So it's a good labor market.

29:50.006 --> 29:51.596 align:middle
And we've seen inflation
come down.

29:51.596 --> 29:52.386 align:middle
We've talked about that.

29:52.386 --> 29:54.826 align:middle
So we've got six months
of good inflation data

29:54.826 --> 29:56.706 align:middle
and an expectation that
there's more to come.

29:56.706 --> 29:59.966 align:middle
So this is a-this
is a good situation.

30:00.176 --> 30:00.696 align:middle
Let's be honest.

30:00.696 --> 30:02.046 align:middle
This is a-this is
a good economy.

30:02.466 --> 30:03.526 align:middle
But what's the outlook?

30:03.526 --> 30:04.006 align:middle
That's looking

30:04.006 --> 30:06.716 align:middle
in the rearview-the outlook-we
do expect growth to moderate.

30:06.716 --> 30:08.876 align:middle
Of course, we have
expected it for some time,

30:08.876 --> 30:12.446 align:middle
and it hasn't happened, but we
do expect that it will moderate

30:12.446 --> 30:15.536 align:middle
as supply chain and labor market
normalization runs its course.

30:16.206 --> 30:19.466 align:middle
The labor market is
rebalancing, as, as I mentioned.

30:19.996 --> 30:21.216 align:middle
Job creation has slowed.

30:21.216 --> 30:23.316 align:middle
The base of job growth
has narrowed.

30:23.876 --> 30:28.656 align:middle
And, of course, 12-month
inflation is, is above target

30:28.656 --> 30:31.096 align:middle
and getting, you know,
getting down closer to target.

30:31.096 --> 30:32.746 align:middle
It's not guaranteed,
but we do seem

30:33.306 --> 30:34.966 align:middle
to be getting on track for that.

30:34.966 --> 30:38.856 align:middle
So those are the risks and, and
questions we have to answer.

30:38.856 --> 30:40.586 align:middle
But, overall, this is
a pretty good picture.

30:40.586 --> 30:41.816 align:middle
It, it is a good picture.

30:42.426 --> 30:44.856 align:middle
Your second question was-sorry.

30:44.856 --> 30:45.246 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

30:46.306 --> 30:50.386 align:middle
Could you get inflation
that is below target-end

30:50.386 --> 30:52.256 align:middle
up with inflation below
target, and you have

30:52.256 --> 30:53.916 align:middle
to do something about that?

30:53.916 --> 30:54.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:55.046 --> 30:57.776 align:middle
So we-the thing is, we're
not looking for inflation

30:57.776 --> 30:59.976 align:middle
to tap the 2 percent base once.

31:00.236 --> 31:04.016 align:middle
We're looking for it to settle
out over time at 2 percent.

31:04.646 --> 31:07.216 align:middle
And the same thing is true
if we have a month or two

31:07.216 --> 31:09.106 align:middle
of lower-and we have
that now-of,

31:09.106 --> 31:10.776 align:middle
of inflation that's annualized

31:10.776 --> 31:12.986 align:middle
at a-at a lower level-that
wouldn't be good.

31:12.986 --> 31:14.556 align:middle
We're not-you know,
we're not looking

31:14.556 --> 31:17.196 align:middle
to have inflation
anchor below 2 percent.

31:17.196 --> 31:18.876 align:middle
We're looking to have
it anchor at 2 percent.

31:18.876 --> 31:21.266 align:middle
So if we do face
those circumstances,

31:21.296 --> 31:22.696 align:middle
then we'll have to
deal with that.

31:22.696 --> 31:26.776 align:middle
I think-I think as of now,
you know, the, the question,

31:26.776 --> 31:28.896 align:middle
which-we want to take
advantage of this situation

31:29.316 --> 31:31.466 align:middle
and finish the job on inflation
while keeping the labor

31:31.466 --> 31:32.076 align:middle
market strong.

31:33.786 --> 31:34.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:35.036 --> 31:36.326 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

31:36.326 --> 31:37.726 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

31:37.726 --> 31:39.766 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking this.

31:39.766 --> 31:42.896 align:middle
So, as I've-as I've heard from
some District Fed presidents,

31:42.896 --> 31:45.386 align:middle
is it, in your view, a
little premature to think

31:45.386 --> 31:47.096 align:middle
that rate cuts are
right around the corner?

31:47.096 --> 31:49.256 align:middle
And then when we do see
that first rate cut,

31:49.496 --> 31:51.626 align:middle
is that-should we interpret
that as the beginning

31:51.626 --> 31:53.486 align:middle
of a rate-cut cycle,
or is it a one-off?

31:54.116 --> 31:54.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:55.106 --> 31:58.796 align:middle
So I'll point you to that
language on your first question.

31:58.796 --> 32:01.986 align:middle
We, we included that
language in the statement

32:02.206 --> 32:06.996 align:middle
to signal clearly
that-with strong growth,

32:07.266 --> 32:09.696 align:middle
strong labor market, inflation
coming down-the Committee

32:09.696 --> 32:13.326 align:middle
intends to move carefully
as we consider when to begin

32:13.326 --> 32:17.086 align:middle
to dial back the restrictive
stance that we have in place.

32:17.636 --> 32:22.606 align:middle
So if you take that to the
current context-current context,

32:22.606 --> 32:23.826 align:middle
we're going to be
data dependent.

32:24.006 --> 32:25.676 align:middle
We're going to be looking
at this meeting by meeting.

32:26.266 --> 32:29.456 align:middle
Based on the meeting
today, I would tell you

32:29.456 --> 32:32.356 align:middle
that I don't think it's likely

32:32.486 --> 32:35.206 align:middle
that the Committee will reach a
level of confidence by the time

32:35.206 --> 32:38.626 align:middle
of the March meeting to identify
March as the time to do that.

32:39.086 --> 32:40.886 align:middle
But that's, that's to be seen.

32:41.586 --> 32:44.576 align:middle
So I wouldn't call-you know,
when you say-when you ask me

32:44.576 --> 32:47.766 align:middle
about "in the near term,"
I'm hearing that as March.

32:47.766 --> 32:50.486 align:middle
I would say I don't
think that's-that's,

32:50.486 --> 32:52.086 align:middle
that's probably not
the most likely case

32:52.086 --> 32:53.466 align:middle
or what we would
call the base case.

32:54.036 --> 32:55.666 align:middle
And your second question
is- EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:55.666 --> 32:58.346 align:middle
On, on the-is this the start
of a-when we see a cut,

32:58.506 --> 33:00.086 align:middle
is it the start of
a cutting cycle,

33:00.396 --> 33:01.536 align:middle
or is it-could it
just be a one-off?

33:01.646 --> 33:02.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:02.406 --> 33:03.776 align:middle
You know, that's going
to depend on the data.

33:03.816 --> 33:06.276 align:middle
The whole thing is, this is
going to depend on the data.

33:06.276 --> 33:08.066 align:middle
We're going to be looking
at the economic data

33:08.446 --> 33:11.106 align:middle
as it affects the outlook
and the balance of risks.

33:11.106 --> 33:13.286 align:middle
And we're going to make our
decisions based on that.

33:13.566 --> 33:16.326 align:middle
And it could wind up-you know,
we'll, we'll have another SEP

33:16.326 --> 33:18.416 align:middle
at the March meeting, and,

33:18.416 --> 33:19.776 align:middle
and people will write
down what they think.

33:19.776 --> 33:21.586 align:middle
But, in the end, it's
really going to depend

33:21.586 --> 33:23.226 align:middle
on how the economy evolves.

33:23.226 --> 33:25.416 align:middle
We talked about, there are
risks that would cause us

33:25.416 --> 33:28.556 align:middle
to go slower-for example,
stronger inflation-more,

33:28.556 --> 33:29.866 align:middle
more persistent inflation.

33:30.266 --> 33:32.736 align:middle
There are risks that would cause
us to-if they happen-that would

33:32.736 --> 33:34.826 align:middle
cause us to go faster
or-and sooner.

33:34.826 --> 33:36.676 align:middle
And that would be a weakening
in the labor market or,

33:36.676 --> 33:40.756 align:middle
for that matter, very, very
persuasive lower inflation.

33:41.366 --> 33:42.306 align:middle
Those are the kinds of things.

33:42.306 --> 33:44.626 align:middle
So we're just-we're just going
to be reacting to the data.

33:44.626 --> 33:46.526 align:middle
That's the-that's really
the only way we can do this.

33:46.616 --> 33:48.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:48.046 --> 33:49.386 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

33:51.636 --> 33:53.116 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

33:53.416 --> 33:56.356 align:middle
Could you talk a little bit
more about productivity growth?

33:56.776 --> 33:59.416 align:middle
You know, you've mentioned
multiple times about, you know,

33:59.416 --> 34:00.976 align:middle
the level of wage
growth that's consistent

34:00.976 --> 34:02.156 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

34:02.156 --> 34:05.126 align:middle
We've obviously seen,
you know-you were talking

34:05.206 --> 34:07.516 align:middle
about ECI this morning, in
which it's cooled a little bit

34:07.516 --> 34:09.806 align:middle
but still sort of above
what you wanted to see.

34:10.106 --> 34:11.516 align:middle
Growth has been very strong.

34:11.516 --> 34:14.666 align:middle
How much of those numbers do
you attribute to productivity?

34:14.846 --> 34:18.926 align:middle
And do you see that productivity
as sort of just temporary

34:18.926 --> 34:20.916 align:middle
because of the factors-the labor

34:20.916 --> 34:23.146 align:middle
and supply chain factors you
were talking about-or do you

34:23.146 --> 34:27.766 align:middle
think that productivity growth
will, will fade over time?

34:28.126 --> 34:28.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:28.596 --> 34:30.546 align:middle
So this is a really
interesting question.

34:30.546 --> 34:34.876 align:middle
And I think-my, my own view
is-I think if you look,

34:34.926 --> 34:40.046 align:middle
look back to the pandemic, you,
you saw a spike in productivity

34:40.046 --> 34:42.086 align:middle
as workers were laid off, and,

34:42.086 --> 34:43.986 align:middle
and activity didn't
decline as fast.

34:44.386 --> 34:47.556 align:middle
And then you saw a deep
trough of productivity.

34:47.556 --> 34:51.416 align:middle
And then, over the last-you saw
high productivity last year,

34:51.416 --> 34:52.286 align:middle
in '23.

34:52.666 --> 34:56.346 align:middle
I think we're, we're basically
in the throes of getting

34:56.346 --> 34:57.766 align:middle
through the pandemic economy.

34:57.766 --> 35:00.416 align:middle
And the question will be, what,

35:00.416 --> 35:02.656 align:middle
what is it that has
changed the-you know,

35:02.656 --> 35:04.516 align:middle
the productivity tends
to be based on, you know,

35:04.516 --> 35:07.286 align:middle
fundamental aspects
of our economy.

35:07.286 --> 35:10.246 align:middle
Is there-is there a case-will
it be the case that we come

35:10.246 --> 35:13.846 align:middle
out of this more productive
on a sustained basis?

35:13.846 --> 35:14.846 align:middle
And I don't know.

35:14.846 --> 35:15.616 align:middle
I don't know.

35:15.616 --> 35:16.346 align:middle
What would it take?

35:16.346 --> 35:18.686 align:middle
It would take-you know,
people talk about AI,

35:19.206 --> 35:23.436 align:middle
but I would-my guess is that
we may shake out and be back

35:23.436 --> 35:26.276 align:middle
where we were, because I
don't-I'm not sure I see-work

35:26.276 --> 35:29.706 align:middle
from home doesn't seem like it's
a big productivity increase.

35:29.706 --> 35:33.806 align:middle
Or AI, artificial
intelligence-generative it may

35:33.806 --> 35:36.366 align:middle
be, but probably not in
the short run-probably,

35:36.366 --> 35:37.276 align:middle
maybe in the longer run.

35:37.576 --> 35:40.606 align:middle
So I'm not-I'm not seeing
why it would, but, you know,

35:40.696 --> 35:42.706 align:middle
right-you know, right
now I would say

35:42.706 --> 35:45.736 align:middle
that productivity is kind
of what falls out of the,

35:46.336 --> 35:48.856 align:middle
the broader forces that are
driving people in and out

35:48.856 --> 35:51.086 align:middle
of the labor force and,
and activity returning

35:51.086 --> 35:52.656 align:middle
and supply chains getting fixed.

35:52.696 --> 35:53.626 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:53.626 --> 35:56.196 align:middle
Right. So would that be
behind why we've seen

35:56.196 --> 35:58.936 align:middle
such strong growth, but we've
also seen inflation fall-that

35:59.056 --> 36:00.826 align:middle
maybe there's just a higher
level of productivity?

36:00.826 --> 36:01.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:01.046 --> 36:01.746 align:middle
That's one way to look at it.

36:01.746 --> 36:04.796 align:middle
Yeah. MICHELLE SMITH.

36:04.966 --> 36:07.626 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

36:07.656 --> 36:08.396 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

36:08.396 --> 36:10.496 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

36:10.676 --> 36:13.426 align:middle
I want to ask a little
bit more about housing.

36:13.736 --> 36:16.536 align:middle
I'm wondering-how closely
are you watching rent

36:16.536 --> 36:19.376 align:middle
and housing prices as
you evaluate whether

36:19.376 --> 36:20.456 align:middle
and when to cut rates?

36:20.456 --> 36:22.766 align:middle
And it seems like housing
prices are not coming

36:22.766 --> 36:25.106 align:middle
down as quickly as you expected.

36:26.206 --> 36:26.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:26.826 --> 36:31.326 align:middle
So when we think
about, you know-our,

36:31.496 --> 36:34.386 align:middle
our statutory goals are maximum
employment and price stability,

36:34.456 --> 36:35.506 align:middle
and that's what we're targeting.

36:35.926 --> 36:37.996 align:middle
We're not targeting
housing price inflation,

36:38.446 --> 36:39.996 align:middle
the cost of housing,
or any of those things.

36:39.996 --> 36:42.266 align:middle
Those are very important
things for people's lives.

36:42.996 --> 36:44.586 align:middle
But they're not-you know,

36:44.586 --> 36:45.836 align:middle
those are not the
things we're targeting.

36:45.886 --> 36:49.236 align:middle
We're also well aware that when
we cut rates at the beginning

36:49.236 --> 36:52.226 align:middle
of the pandemic, for
example, the housing,

36:52.436 --> 36:54.836 align:middle
housing industry was helped
more than any other industry.

36:54.966 --> 36:57.846 align:middle
And when we raise rates, the
housing industry can be hurt,

36:57.846 --> 37:00.256 align:middle
because it's a very
interest-sensitive sector.

37:01.236 --> 37:03.666 align:middle
On top of that, we have
longer-run problems

37:03.666 --> 37:05.176 align:middle
with the availability
of housing.

37:05.796 --> 37:08.846 align:middle
You know, we have a, a built-up
set of cities, and, and,

37:08.846 --> 37:10.486 align:middle
you know, people are moving
further and further out.

37:10.486 --> 37:13.146 align:middle
So there's-there hasn't
been enough housing built.

37:13.566 --> 37:15.006 align:middle
And these are not-these
are not things

37:15.006 --> 37:16.406 align:middle
that we have any
tools to address.

37:16.896 --> 37:20.106 align:middle
But, you know, where it comes
into play very specifically

37:20.626 --> 37:23.946 align:middle
in our work is inflation,
which is a combination.

37:23.946 --> 37:26.066 align:middle
It's, it's really
rental inflation.

37:26.066 --> 37:28.146 align:middle
You're taking owners'
equivalent rent

37:28.146 --> 37:30.086 align:middle
and then actual rent
paid by tenants.

37:30.086 --> 37:34.226 align:middle
And you're, you're running that
through the CPI calculation.

37:34.226 --> 37:36.206 align:middle
Or the PCE [inflation]
calculation-the one we look at.

37:36.696 --> 37:41.006 align:middle
And what that's telling you is
that market rents are increasing

37:41.006 --> 37:43.906 align:middle
at a much lower rate or
even being flat and that

37:43.906 --> 37:46.006 align:middle
that will show up in
inflation over time.

37:46.006 --> 37:48.656 align:middle
It has to as long as
that remains the case.

37:48.876 --> 37:50.086 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

37:50.086 --> 37:52.126 align:middle
And just real quick-what is
your response to the letter

37:52.126 --> 37:53.706 align:middle
that was sent to
you by some members

37:53.706 --> 37:57.326 align:middle
of Congress asking the Fed
to lower interest rates

37:57.326 --> 37:58.566 align:middle
to make housing more affordable?

37:58.566 --> 37:58.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:59.156 --> 38:02.506 align:middle
My response is what I started
with, which is that our,

38:02.796 --> 38:06.616 align:middle
our job-the job Congress has
given us is price stability

38:06.616 --> 38:07.646 align:middle
and maximum employment.

38:08.206 --> 38:11.686 align:middle
Price stability is absolutely
essential for people's lives,

38:11.686 --> 38:14.346 align:middle
most importantly for-well,
not most importantly-most,

38:14.666 --> 38:18.406 align:middle
mostly for people at the lower
end of the income spectrum

38:18.406 --> 38:21.286 align:middle
who are living at the
edges-at the margins.

38:21.286 --> 38:24.136 align:middle
And so someone-for someone
like that, high inflation

38:24.136 --> 38:27.906 align:middle
in the-in the necessities
of life-right away,

38:27.906 --> 38:30.326 align:middle
you're in trouble, whereas even
middle-class people have some,

38:30.906 --> 38:34.506 align:middle
you know, some scope
to absorb higher costs.

38:34.506 --> 38:36.266 align:middle
So we have to get-it's our job.

38:36.266 --> 38:39.616 align:middle
It's what society has asked us
to do, is to get inflation down,

38:39.616 --> 38:42.486 align:middle
and the tools that we use
to do it are interest rates.

38:42.906 --> 38:44.746 align:middle
So that's how we
think about that.

38:44.746 --> 38:45.776 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:45.966 --> 38:46.206 align:middle
Courtenay.

38:46.526 --> 38:47.476 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

38:49.396 --> 38:50.866 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

38:50.946 --> 38:52.686 align:middle
Can you give us some insight

38:52.686 --> 38:55.536 align:middle
into whether the Committee
discussed the possibility

38:55.536 --> 38:57.896 align:middle
of slowing balance sheet
runoff in the months ahead?

38:59.336 --> 38:59.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:00.836 --> 39:08.836 align:middle
Yes. So I would start by saying
that balance sheet runoff

39:08.836 --> 39:10.726 align:middle
so far has gone very well.

39:11.326 --> 39:14.416 align:middle
And as the process has
continued, you know,

39:14.416 --> 39:18.206 align:middle
we're getting to that time where
questions are beginning to come

39:18.266 --> 39:20.776 align:middle
into greater focus about the
pace of runoff and all that.

39:20.776 --> 39:23.126 align:middle
So at this meeting, we
did have some discussion

39:23.126 --> 39:25.036 align:middle
of the balance sheet,
and we're planning

39:25.036 --> 39:29.536 align:middle
to begin in-depth discussions
of balance sheet issues

39:29.536 --> 39:30.736 align:middle
at our next meeting in March.

39:31.086 --> 39:33.766 align:middle
So those, those questions are
all coming into scope now,

39:33.766 --> 39:34.706 align:middle
and we're focusing on them.

39:34.706 --> 39:35.996 align:middle
But we're, we're
at the beginning

39:35.996 --> 39:36.896 align:middle
of that process, I would say.

39:36.896 --> 39:37.486 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

39:37.486 --> 39:40.536 align:middle
Quick follow-up-is it the
case that the Fed would decide

39:40.536 --> 39:42.916 align:middle
to lower rates and
make adjustments

39:42.916 --> 39:44.716 align:middle
to the balance sheet
runoff in tandem?

39:46.156 --> 39:46.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:46.816 --> 39:49.076 align:middle
Yes, we do-we see those
as independent tools.

39:49.256 --> 39:51.256 align:middle
And so they don't-for example,

39:51.256 --> 39:53.126 align:middle
if you're-if you're
normalizing policy,

39:53.546 --> 39:55.856 align:middle
you might be reducing
rates but continuing

39:55.856 --> 39:56.896 align:middle
to run off the balance sheet.

39:57.186 --> 39:58.766 align:middle
In both cases, that's
normalization,

39:58.766 --> 40:01.136 align:middle
but from a strict
monetary policy standpoint,

40:01.136 --> 40:02.926 align:middle
you could say we're loosening
along with tightening.

40:02.926 --> 40:04.486 align:middle
So that, that could happen.

40:04.766 --> 40:06.726 align:middle
It's not something we're
planning or thinking about,

40:06.766 --> 40:09.066 align:middle
but right now, we're
thinking about getting

40:09.066 --> 40:10.646 align:middle
to a place where-we're going

40:10.646 --> 40:12.216 align:middle
to see the balance sheet
runoff to continue.

40:12.216 --> 40:15.496 align:middle
We're watching it
carefully, and, as I said,

40:15.496 --> 40:16.526 align:middle
we'll-we'll be looking into that

40:16.526 --> 40:17.756 align:middle
as a Committee starting
in March.

40:18.296 --> 40:19.646 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

40:19.936 --> 40:21.506 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

40:21.586 --> 40:23.446 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

40:23.446 --> 40:25.116 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

40:25.116 --> 40:26.226 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

40:26.766 --> 40:29.236 align:middle
You've mentioned six good
months of inflation data,

40:29.236 --> 40:30.986 align:middle
but that not being enough
to build up confidence.

40:31.526 --> 40:33.046 align:middle
Based on your previous response

40:33.046 --> 40:35.666 align:middle
that your base case is you
probably wouldn't start easing

40:35.666 --> 40:37.636 align:middle
yet in March, the implication is

40:37.636 --> 40:39.896 align:middle
that eight good months
might not be enough, either.

40:40.276 --> 40:42.696 align:middle
Roughly how many months do
you think you might need of,

40:42.726 --> 40:44.866 align:middle
of good inflation data
to be-to be confident?

40:44.866 --> 40:45.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:45.306 --> 40:47.556 align:middle
I'm, I'm not in a position
to put a number on it.

40:47.556 --> 40:50.186 align:middle
I'm just going to
say-and it's not

40:50.186 --> 40:51.546 align:middle
that we don't have
any confidence.

40:51.546 --> 40:55.076 align:middle
We, we have growing
confidence, but not to the point

40:55.076 --> 40:58.966 align:middle
where we-where we feel like-it's
a highly consequential decision

40:58.966 --> 41:03.136 align:middle
to start the process of, of
dialing back on restriction.

41:03.136 --> 41:04.116 align:middle
And we want to get that right,

41:04.116 --> 41:06.356 align:middle
and we feel like the strong
economy, strong labor market,

41:06.356 --> 41:08.686 align:middle
inflation coming down-it gives
us the ability to do that.

41:08.886 --> 41:11.906 align:middle
We think that's the best way we
can serve the public, because,

41:12.356 --> 41:16.316 align:middle
ultimately, we, we've made a
lot of progress on inflation.

41:16.316 --> 41:17.196 align:middle
We just want to make sure

41:17.196 --> 41:19.676 align:middle
that we do get the job
done in a sustainable way.

41:19.956 --> 41:20.976 align:middle
That's how we're
thinking about it.

41:21.296 --> 41:23.516 align:middle
In terms of when that'll be,
you know, that, that'll all come

41:23.516 --> 41:26.356 align:middle
out of our communications,
and, you know,

41:26.686 --> 41:29.056 align:middle
we won't-we won't
keep that a secret.

41:29.936 --> 41:30.776 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:31.176 --> 41:33.066 align:middle
Evan. EVAN RYSER.

41:34.576 --> 41:35.696 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:35.696 --> 41:37.246 align:middle
Evan Ryser with MNI Market News.

41:37.726 --> 41:40.416 align:middle
Can you explain a
little bit more

41:40.416 --> 41:43.706 align:middle
on what you're considering
when tapering QT?

41:43.706 --> 41:47.596 align:middle
Do you need to see the overnight
reverse repo facility all the

41:47.596 --> 41:50.166 align:middle
way down to zero, or is it
something that you can start

41:50.496 --> 41:52.306 align:middle
with a couple hundred
billion dollars there?

41:52.306 --> 41:53.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:53.626 --> 41:56.046 align:middle
Not a decision that we've made,
but I, I wouldn't think we'd,

41:56.216 --> 41:57.656 align:middle
we'd be-we wouldn't
be taking a position

41:57.656 --> 41:58.566 align:middle
that it's got to go to zero.

41:58.566 --> 42:00.616 align:middle
I mean, if it-if it
were to stabilize

42:00.616 --> 42:01.396 align:middle
at a different level-but that's,

42:01.436 --> 42:02.646 align:middle
that's not a decision
that we've made.

42:02.646 --> 42:03.736 align:middle
That's, that's what
we'll be talking

42:03.736 --> 42:04.726 align:middle
about at the March meeting.

42:05.086 --> 42:08.306 align:middle
A whole range of issues
will be briefed up,

42:08.306 --> 42:11.106 align:middle
and the Committee will get
into-get into all of the issues

42:11.266 --> 42:13.096 align:middle
that will be arising over
the course of the next,

42:13.096 --> 42:14.486 align:middle
let's say, year or so.

42:16.686 --> 42:17.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:17.566 --> 42:19.356 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

42:19.706 --> 42:21.106 align:middle
Thanks. Greg Robb
from MarketWatch.

42:21.806 --> 42:24.246 align:middle
Chair Powell, I want to
change gears a little bit.

42:24.366 --> 42:28.106 align:middle
In the presidential primary
campaign that's been going

42:28.106 --> 42:31.876 align:middle
on for the last nine months or
so, your name has come up often,

42:32.126 --> 42:34.386 align:middle
and many Republican
candidates had said

42:34.706 --> 42:37.436 align:middle
that they probably wouldn't
want to give you a third term.

42:37.796 --> 42:39.916 align:middle
So I wanted to give you a
chance to talk about that.

42:40.086 --> 42:43.386 align:middle
Do you want another
term-you've had-on the Fed?

42:43.426 --> 42:45.276 align:middle
What, what's your
stance on that?

42:45.276 --> 42:45.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:45.636 --> 42:46.806 align:middle
I don't have a stance on that.

42:47.176 --> 42:48.456 align:middle
It's not something
I'm focused on.

42:48.456 --> 42:49.786 align:middle
[We're] focused on
doing our jobs.

42:49.976 --> 42:53.646 align:middle
We have-this year is going to be
a highly consequential year for,

42:54.066 --> 42:55.476 align:middle
for the Fed and for
monetary policy.

42:55.476 --> 42:57.316 align:middle
And we're, all of us,
very buckled down,

42:57.356 --> 42:58.406 align:middle
focused on doing our jobs.

43:00.036 --> 43:01.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:01.576 --> 43:05.746 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

43:07.046 --> 43:07.886 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

43:07.886 --> 43:09.676 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

43:10.536 --> 43:12.626 align:middle
As you mentioned, core PCE
[inflation] has been running

43:12.626 --> 43:15.696 align:middle
at 1.9 percent over
the past six months.

43:16.116 --> 43:19.056 align:middle
And you guys are actually
expecting core inflation higher

43:19.056 --> 43:20.776 align:middle
this year, at 2.4 percent,

43:20.776 --> 43:22.286 align:middle
compared to that
six-month measure.

43:22.836 --> 43:24.946 align:middle
Given that forecast
and that the median is

43:24.946 --> 43:29.096 align:middle
for three rate cuts this year,
what happens if inflation stays

43:29.096 --> 43:31.596 align:middle
where it's been over
the last six months

43:31.876 --> 43:32.876 align:middle
for the next six months?

43:33.626 --> 43:33.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:34.216 --> 43:36.486 align:middle
So I-you know, we're going
to do-we'll update our,

43:36.486 --> 43:38.646 align:middle
our inflation forecasts
at the next meeting.

43:38.646 --> 43:40.426 align:middle
You referred to the
December meeting.

43:40.426 --> 43:42.506 align:middle
That's, that's, you know,
three months old [by March],

43:42.506 --> 43:44.826 align:middle
so it might be lower now,
given the data we've gotten.

43:45.296 --> 43:46.756 align:middle
So, look, as I mentioned,

43:46.756 --> 43:48.016 align:middle
we're going to be
reacting to the data.

43:48.406 --> 43:52.156 align:middle
If, if we get-if we get very
strong inflation data and it,

43:52.156 --> 43:52.916 align:middle
it kicks back up,

43:53.026 --> 43:55.536 align:middle
then it'll-then we'll go
slower or later or both.

43:56.256 --> 43:59.996 align:middle
If we got really good inflation
data soon, that would matter

43:59.996 --> 44:03.226 align:middle
for both the-that, that
would tell us that,

44:03.266 --> 44:05.616 align:middle
that we could go sooner
and perhaps go faster.

44:05.866 --> 44:07.546 align:middle
So we're just going
to be-but, of course,

44:07.546 --> 44:09.986 align:middle
we'll weigh that with
all the other factors.

44:09.986 --> 44:13.046 align:middle
We're setting policy based on
the totality of, of the data.

44:13.046 --> 44:13.766 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

44:14.316 --> 44:17.556 align:middle
But just to follow-if inflation
stays where it is currently,

44:17.796 --> 44:18.686 align:middle
that would probably mean

44:18.686 --> 44:21.556 align:middle
that the real interest rate
becomes more restrictive.

44:21.556 --> 44:23.536 align:middle
Would that mean you'd have
to trim more, perhaps,

44:23.606 --> 44:24.686 align:middle
than you already
have factored in?

44:24.686 --> 44:24.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:24.976 --> 44:27.246 align:middle
Well, I think if we-if
we came to the view that,

44:27.416 --> 44:30.266 align:middle
that inflation were-that the
six-month inflation numbers,

44:30.266 --> 44:32.686 align:middle
which are very close
to 2 [percent], were,

44:32.926 --> 44:36.456 align:middle
in "PCE world"-if we came
to-if that's-if we thought

44:36.456 --> 44:38.196 align:middle
that is really where we're
going to be, then, yes,

44:38.196 --> 44:39.596 align:middle
our policy would be
in a different place.

44:39.826 --> 44:42.516 align:middle
It would. But, you know, that's
the whole point is, we're trying

44:42.516 --> 44:46.246 align:middle
to get comfortable
and gain confidence

44:46.556 --> 44:48.436 align:middle
that that is where-that
inflation is

44:48.436 --> 44:52.296 align:middle
on a sustainable path down to
2 percent or toward 2 percent.

44:53.416 --> 44:54.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:54.386 --> 44:57.606 align:middle
Daniel. DANIEL AVIS.

44:58.416 --> 44:59.306 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

44:59.306 --> 45:00.776 align:middle
Daniel Avis, Agence
France-Presse.

45:00.776 --> 45:03.186 align:middle
I just wondered if I
could get your comment

45:03.186 --> 45:05.176 align:middle
on the recent consumer
confidence data.

45:05.176 --> 45:07.196 align:middle
It seems to suggest
that consumers are sort

45:07.346 --> 45:10.286 align:middle
of moving towards a much more
optimistic view of the economy.

45:10.616 --> 45:13.396 align:middle
I just wonder-is it fair to
say that they're moving towards

45:13.586 --> 45:16.176 align:middle
where the Fed appears to
have been in recent months?

45:16.236 --> 45:18.336 align:middle
And, you know, do you
think that inflation

45:18.336 --> 45:20.856 align:middle
and falling inflation perhaps
has played a role in that?

45:20.856 --> 45:22.816 align:middle
And what challenges do
you see, going forward?

45:22.946 --> 45:23.266 align:middle
Thank you.

45:23.436 --> 45:24.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:24.406 --> 45:26.446 align:middle
Yeah, so it's been-it's
been interesting

45:26.446 --> 45:29.336 align:middle
that confidence surveys
have been weak,

45:29.916 --> 45:32.996 align:middle
at a time when unemployment
has been low-very low,

45:32.996 --> 45:34.766 align:middle
historically low-for
a couple of years.

45:34.766 --> 45:36.356 align:middle
And-but, nonetheless,
that's been the case.

45:36.806 --> 45:38.316 align:middle
And we've asked ourselves
why that is.

45:38.316 --> 45:40.866 align:middle
And, you know, one obvious
answer-we don't pretend

45:40.866 --> 45:42.026 align:middle
to have perfect wisdom

45:42.026 --> 45:44.736 align:middle
on this-is-but one
obvious answer is

45:44.736 --> 45:45.956 align:middle
that the price level is high.

45:45.956 --> 45:50.226 align:middle
So prices went up much more
than 2 percent for year-per year

45:50.226 --> 45:51.786 align:middle
for a couple of years.

45:51.786 --> 45:53.156 align:middle
And people are going
to the store,

45:53.676 --> 45:56.506 align:middle
and they're paying much
more for the basics of life

45:56.506 --> 45:59.156 align:middle
than they were two years
ago-three years ago.

45:59.576 --> 46:00.766 align:middle
And they're not happy about it.

46:00.766 --> 46:03.146 align:middle
And it's fine that
inflation is coming down,

46:03.146 --> 46:04.976 align:middle
but the price-the prices
they're paying are still high.

46:04.976 --> 46:08.216 align:middle
So that, that is what-that,
that has to be some part

46:08.786 --> 46:10.106 align:middle
of why people are unhappy.

46:10.106 --> 46:11.476 align:middle
And they're, they're
right to be unhappy.

46:11.476 --> 46:14.096 align:middle
You know, this is why we
need to keep price stability.

46:14.096 --> 46:16.936 align:middle
It's why we need to do our
jobs-so that people don't have

46:16.936 --> 46:18.016 align:middle
to deal with things like this.

46:18.756 --> 46:20.386 align:middle
In terms of [surveys]-you're
right.

46:20.386 --> 46:24.506 align:middle
In, in recent, recent surveys,
a couple of-you've seen a couple

46:24.506 --> 46:30.336 align:middle
of significant increases in,
in consumer confidence or,

46:30.386 --> 46:31.746 align:middle
or happiness with the economy.

46:32.236 --> 46:34.016 align:middle
I guess that's a good thing.

46:34.016 --> 46:36.296 align:middle
That can-that can support
spending-can support

46:36.296 --> 46:37.296 align:middle
economic activity.

46:37.606 --> 46:38.686 align:middle
There's some evidence of that.

46:39.146 --> 46:41.586 align:middle
But it is-it is a fact that
we have seen, you know,

46:41.586 --> 46:42.606 align:middle
a meaningful increase.

46:42.766 --> 46:45.726 align:middle
I think levels of confidence
are still maybe not as high

46:45.726 --> 46:46.996 align:middle
as they've been at
various times.

46:47.036 --> 46:48.516 align:middle
But it's-they certainly
have come up.

46:50.426 --> 46:51.596 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:51.856 --> 46:52.706 align:middle
Bryan. BRYAN MENA.

46:53.516 --> 46:57.676 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

46:57.736 --> 46:58.806 align:middle
Bryan Mena, CNN Business.

46:59.526 --> 47:01.336 align:middle
Committee members have
said they'd like to meet

47:01.336 --> 47:03.496 align:middle
with business leaders and
stakeholders in person

47:03.496 --> 47:05.936 align:middle
to learn more about the
economy in real time,

47:05.936 --> 47:08.356 align:middle
given that some data is
subject to large revisions,

47:08.406 --> 47:10.066 align:middle
the issue of seasonal
adjustments being thrown off

47:10.066 --> 47:13.066 align:middle
balance, and many readings of
the economy being quarterly.

47:13.476 --> 47:15.866 align:middle
So did any members say they've
learned anything not reflected

47:15.866 --> 47:16.556 align:middle
in the data?

47:16.556 --> 47:17.786 align:middle
Or have you yourself
learned anything

47:17.786 --> 47:19.466 align:middle
through anecdotal evidence

47:19.466 --> 47:20.926 align:middle
that hasn't been
captured in the data yet?

47:21.686 --> 47:22.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:22.416 --> 47:23.016 align:middle
Well, yes.

47:23.416 --> 47:25.306 align:middle
I'm, I'm a big believer
that-yes.

47:25.306 --> 47:28.466 align:middle
So we, we do meet with
outside groups who come

47:28.466 --> 47:30.206 align:middle
from all different
parts of the economy.

47:30.836 --> 47:35.406 align:middle
And I always feel like you-I
mean, I spent most of my life

47:35.406 --> 47:36.556 align:middle
in the private sector looking

47:36.556 --> 47:39.426 align:middle
at companies-individual
companies-individual management

47:39.426 --> 47:41.616 align:middle
teams-and then building
out from that.

47:41.616 --> 47:45.966 align:middle
And so, starting with
GDP data is-and working

47:45.966 --> 47:48.046 align:middle
into what's actually
affecting people's lives

47:48.456 --> 47:50.006 align:middle
is-is challenging.

47:50.006 --> 47:50.606 align:middle
It's very hard.

47:50.606 --> 47:52.386 align:middle
So I, I really like
anecdotal data.

47:52.386 --> 47:54.016 align:middle
In addition, as you know,

47:54.346 --> 47:59.226 align:middle
the 12 Reserve Banks have really
the best network of anyone.

47:59.486 --> 48:02.236 align:middle
In all their Districts,
they're talking to, you know,

48:02.236 --> 48:05.246 align:middle
not just the business community,
but the educational, medical,

48:05.846 --> 48:08.546 align:middle
all, all-you know,
nonprofit community.

48:09.046 --> 48:10.566 align:middle
They have arms into all of that.

48:10.686 --> 48:12.306 align:middle
And so when they come
back-that's what goes

48:12.306 --> 48:13.136 align:middle
into the Beige Book.

48:13.596 --> 48:16.316 align:middle
But they come back, and what
each Reserve Bank president does

48:16.316 --> 48:18.476 align:middle
is, during the outlook
go-around,

48:18.956 --> 48:20.286 align:middle
they'll say, "In my District .

48:20.456 --> 48:23.556 align:middle
. ." And they'll talk about
100 conversations they-not-they

48:23.556 --> 48:26.986 align:middle
won't talk-they, they
will give you input,

48:27.036 --> 48:29.866 align:middle
based on 100 conversations
that they've had with people

48:29.866 --> 48:30.986 align:middle
of all different walks.

48:30.986 --> 48:34.946 align:middle
And it's-I personally
find it very helpful

48:34.946 --> 48:37.796 align:middle
in understanding
what's going on.

48:37.796 --> 48:40.316 align:middle
And also, I think you hear
things before they show

48:40.316 --> 48:41.276 align:middle
up in the data sometimes.

48:42.046 --> 48:42.316 align:middle
BRYAN MENA.

48:42.316 --> 48:45.186 align:middle
Did any of them-did any of them
notice slowing economy based

48:45.186 --> 48:47.036 align:middle
on what they've heard
from, like, their District?

48:47.036 --> 48:47.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:47.456 --> 48:48.786 align:middle
Yes. I mean, if you-if
you look back

48:48.786 --> 48:51.376 align:middle
at the last-not this Beige
Book, but the one before,

48:51.656 --> 48:54.786 align:middle
it was more-there was a
lot of "slower activity."

48:55.026 --> 48:57.656 align:middle
I think that, that what
you're hearing now is, is,

48:57.656 --> 48:58.636 align:middle
things are picking up a bit.

48:59.036 --> 49:01.736 align:middle
You're hearing-not, not in
every District and not every,

49:01.886 --> 49:04.186 align:middle
every person that we talk
to, but you're-overall,

49:04.716 --> 49:08.376 align:middle
it feels like you're hearing
things picking up at the margin.

49:09.396 --> 49:10.726 align:middle
So that's what comes through.

49:10.726 --> 49:11.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:11.916 --> 49:14.156 align:middle
Let's go to Jeff Cox
for the last question.

49:14.866 --> 49:15.706 align:middle
JEFF COX. Thank you, sir.

49:15.706 --> 49:17.186 align:middle
Jeff Cox from CNBC.com.

49:17.416 --> 49:19.946 align:middle
Just kind of looking
to put it all together:

49:20.426 --> 49:23.896 align:middle
You talked about basically
the, the economy looking strong

49:23.896 --> 49:27.266 align:middle
with 3.3 percent annualized
growth in the fourth quarter.

49:27.706 --> 49:31.176 align:middle
Does the strength of the economy
speak more loudly to you now

49:31.336 --> 49:33.556 align:middle
than any inflation threat might?

49:33.626 --> 49:35.656 align:middle
That, you know-you're in a
position, in other words,

49:35.926 --> 49:39.226 align:middle
to keep rates elevated as long
as the economy stays strong,

49:39.226 --> 49:41.696 align:middle
and you're more-you're
more tilted towards that.

49:41.696 --> 49:43.496 align:middle
And also, perhaps,
are you worried at all

49:43.496 --> 49:46.116 align:middle
that the economy is maybe a
little too strong right now

49:46.116 --> 49:48.076 align:middle
and that inflation could
come back at some point?

49:48.076 --> 49:48.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:48.326 --> 49:49.496 align:middle
I'm not so worried about that.

49:49.496 --> 49:52.556 align:middle
You know, it's-again,
we've had inflation come

49:52.556 --> 49:57.386 align:middle
down without a slow economy
and without, you know,

49:57.386 --> 49:59.336 align:middle
important increases
in, in unemployment,

49:59.336 --> 50:01.156 align:middle
and there's no reason why we
should want to get in the way

50:01.156 --> 50:03.216 align:middle
of that process, if
it's going to continue.

50:03.216 --> 50:07.746 align:middle
So I, I am-you know, I
think-I think declining

50:07.746 --> 50:11.346 align:middle
inflation-continued
declines in inflation are,

50:12.496 --> 50:14.126 align:middle
are really the main
thing we're looking at.

50:14.126 --> 50:16.286 align:middle
Of course, we want the labor
market to remain strong, too.

50:16.446 --> 50:17.726 align:middle
We don't have a growth mandate.

50:17.726 --> 50:20.726 align:middle
We've got a, a
maximum-employment mandate

50:20.726 --> 50:22.836 align:middle
and a, a price-stability
mandate,

50:22.836 --> 50:23.996 align:middle
and those are the two
things we look at.

50:23.996 --> 50:24.936 align:middle
Growth only matters

50:24.936 --> 50:27.376 align:middle
to the extent it
influences our achievement

50:28.036 --> 50:39.206 align:middle
of those two-of those
two mandates.

50:40.786 --> 50:46.626 align:middle
Thank you very much.

